WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.780
Push a small current into a resting cell.

2
00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.480
Does the voltage jump to its new level?

3
00:00:09.500 --> 00:00:13.760
In current clamp, you set the current flowing into the cell, and you record its

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:14.760
voltage.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:17.740
Give our cell a small negative step of current.

6
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.080
It pushes the voltage down, away from threshold.

7
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:25.593
So does the voltage jump, the instant the current switches on?

8
00:00:31.567 --> 00:00:35.027
No. It starts moving at once, but smoothly.

9
00:00:36.047 --> 00:00:38.507
Steeply at first, then bending over toward a plateau.

10
00:00:39.650 --> 00:00:43.970
When the step ends, it relaxes back to rest along the same kind of curve.

11
00:00:44.983 --> 00:00:47.383
The reason is that the membrane stores charge.

12
00:00:48.263 --> 00:00:51.743
The thin bilayer between two salty solutions is a capacitor.

13
00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:55.913
The leak channels are resistors, side by side with it.

14
00:00:56.833 --> 00:00:58.913
Picture a straight-sided tank with a drain.

15
00:00:59.793 --> 00:01:02.433
The water level is the size of the voltage change.

16
00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:04.993
The tank's width is the capacitance.

17
00:01:06.017 --> 00:01:09.257
The drain is the leak, and its outflow grows with the level.

18
00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:12.417
The injected current is a hose pouring in.

19
00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:17.250
At first the level has not risen, so nothing extra drains away.

20
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.770
All the inflow raises the level.

21
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:24.463
That sets the starting slope: current divided by capacitance.

22
00:01:25.417 --> 00:01:30.037
As the level rises, more water drains out, so the level rises more slowly.

23
00:01:31.100 --> 00:01:34.160
stops rising when the drain carries all the inflow.

24
00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:35.880
That is the plateau.

25
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:38.193
Now back to the ropes.

26
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:42.113
The injected current is a steady extra pull on the marker.

27
00:01:43.183 --> 00:01:45.923
The ropes stretch until together they cancel it.

28
00:01:46.423 --> 00:01:49.943
So the balance point shifts by the current divided by the total leak,

29
00:01:50.917 --> 00:01:54.257
That shift for each unit of current is the input resistance.

30
00:01:55.157 --> 00:01:56.697
It is one over the total leak.

31
00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:00.170
For our cell, that is 100 MΩ.

32
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.930
The balance point moves at once.

33
00:02:03.730 --> 00:02:06.970
The voltage follows it, at a pace set by capacitance and leak.

34
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:09.823
That pace is the time constant.

35
00:02:10.723 --> 00:02:13.243
It equals capacitance divided by total leak.

36
00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:18.593
In one time constant, the voltage gets about 63% % of the way.

37
00:02:19.513 --> 00:02:20.513
Not all of it.

38
00:02:21.117 --> 00:02:24.057
For our cell, the time constant is 20 ms.

39
00:02:25.250 --> 00:02:27.790
More leak means a smaller change, reached sooner.

40
00:02:29.950 --> 00:02:32.610
A change also lingers after its input ends.

41
00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:36.130
So inputs close together in time overlap and add.

42
00:02:37.133 --> 00:02:38.733
That is temporal summation.

43
00:02:39.553 --> 00:02:42.633
A longer time constant widens the window for overlap.

44
00:02:43.667 --> 00:02:47.887
The total leak sets how big a nudge is, and how fast the voltage gets there.
